      "text": "Lecture slides for EE2001"
    },
    {
      "page": 2,
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Why Fourier series?"
    },
    {
      "page": 2,
      "type": "list_item",
      "text": "Any periodic signal is a sum of sinusoids"
    },
    {
      "page": 2,
      "type": "list_item",
      "text": "Each harmonic has its own amplitude and phase"
    },
    {
      "page": 2,
      "type": "list_item",
      "text": "A filter acts on each harmonic separately"
    },
    {
      "page": 3,
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Worked example"
    },
    {
      "page": 3,
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A square wave at 50 Hz has only odd harmonics."
    },
    {
      "page": 3,
      "type": "list_item",
      "text": "Third harmonic: 150 Hz"
    },
    {
      "page": 3,
      "type": "list_item",
      "text": "Fifth harmonic: 250 Hz"
    },
    {
      "page": 3,
      "type": "list_item",
      "text": "Seventh harmonic: 350 Hz"
    },
    {
      "page": 4,
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Summary"
    },
    {
      "page": 4,
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Periodic signals can be analysed one harmonic at a time."
    },
    {
      "page": 4,
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Next week: the Fourier transform for signals that do not repeat."
    }
  ]
